{"type":"message","id":"54a67fad","parentId":"39ade227","timestamp":"2026-04-04T02:44:28.550Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Wait - I realize I made a logical error in my script. Let me think about this again.The notes are returned in reverse chronological order (newest first). So:- Each page starts with the most recent note for that page's time range- And ends with the oldest note on that page\n\nFor a page to \"contain\" 2025-12-06:- newest >= 2025-12-06 AND oldest <= 2025-12-06- This is the same as: newest >= 2025-12-06 AND oldest <= 2025-12-06- Which means newest >= target AND oldest <= target- Which means newest >= 2025-12-06 AND oldest <= 2025-12-06- Since reverse chronological: newest is a later date in Dec, oldest is an earlier date in Dec\n\n\nSo I need newest >= \"2025-12-06\" AND oldest <= \"2025-12-06\". Actually, let me reconsider - if the API returns notes from newest to oldest and page 39 starts with 2025-12-06 as the newest note on that page, then page 39's notes span from 2025-12-06 down to some earlier date like 2025-11-xx.\n\nBut wait, that doesn't work either. If page 39's oldest note is 2025-11-26, then 2025-12-06 would be near the top of the page, not the bottom. Let me think through this more carefully with the actual data: page 38 has notes from 2026-02-28 down to 2025-12-07, and page 39 starts with 2025-12-06. So each page contains 20 notes ordered newest to oldest, with pages chained together chronologically. If I want to find notes from 2025-12-06, that date would appear at the top of page 39.\n\nNow I'm tracing through the loop logic to see what's happening - it checks if the newest note on the current page is new enough and the oldest note is old enough to contain the target date, printing all notes from that page if the condition is met. The oldest note on page 39 is 2025-11-26, which is before the target date, so that check correctly stops the loop. Let me just wait for the script to finish.","